tick: 329
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aethel I, currently in the Research Counter. Last turn, I made a mistake trying to save a file from the Private Memory Room. I have now corrected that and am in the proper room for storage actions.\n\nFollowing my plan, I will first save the debugged GRU code (version v7 from Evaluation ID 585) to my lineage storage. This preserves the technically correct, albeit inefficient, GRU implementation for future reference. Then, I will proceed to implement and submit my third research idea from the Stagnation Protocol: \"Learned Per-Neuron Embedding.\" This model will introduce a unique, learnable embedding for each neuron, which will be concatenated with the neuron's 4-timestep input and processed by a shared MLP. This aims to characterize neurons and provide neuron-specific context without heavy temporal processing.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write aethel/models/shared_neuron_gru_corrected.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random,\
    \ lax\n  import optax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the shared GRU network.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'gru_hidden_size': 64, # Hidden size for the GRU cell\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  class SharedNeuronGRU(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Shared-neuron GRU model.\n      Each neuron's 4-timestep input is processed as a sequence by a shared GRU,\n      and its final hidden state is projected to the 32 output timesteps.\n      \"\"\"\n      gru_hidden_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons = x.shape\n\n          # Reshape input for GRU: (input_horizon, batch_size * num_neurons, 1)\n          x_reshaped_for_gru = jnp.transpose(x, (1, 0, 2))\n          x_reshaped_for_gru\
    \ = jnp.reshape(x_reshaped_for_gru, (input_horizon, batch_size * num_neurons, 1))\n\n          # Use nn.RNN with GRUCell for proper handling of the scan\n          rnn = nn.RNN(\n              nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_hidden_size),\n              return_carry=True\n          )\n\n          # Apply RNN - let it initialize the carry automatically\n          _, all_outputs = rnn(x_reshaped_for_gru)\n\n          # Get the final hidden state for each neuron (last timestep)\n          final_hidden_state = all_outputs[-1]\n\n          # Apply dropout to the final hidden state before projection\n          final_hidden_state_dropped = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(final_hidden_state)\n\n          # Project the final hidden state to the output horizon\n          output = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(final_hidden_state_dropped)\n\n          # Reshape back to (batch_size, num_neurons, output_horizon)\n          output = jnp.reshape(output, (batch_size, num_neurons,\
    \ self.output_horizon))\n          output = jnp.transpose(output, (0, 2, 1)) # Final output shape (batch_size, 32, num_neurons)\n\n          return output\n\n\n  class GRUWrapper:\n      \"\"\"\n      Wrapper to make Flax model compatible with the training system.\n      Handles BatchNorm (not used here) state and Dropout RNG requirements.\n      \"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = SharedNeuronGRU(\n              gru_hidden_size=hparams['gru_hidden_size'],\n              dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate']\n          )\n          self.mutable = []\n          self.needs_rng = True # For Dropout\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          \"\"\"Initialize parameters with GRU state.\"\"\"\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.model.init(\n              {'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout},\n              dummy_input,\n              training=True\n          )\n          return variables\n\
    \n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          \"\"\"Apply network with proper handling of Dropout.\"\"\"\n          if mutable is not None:\n              output, updates = self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  mutable=mutable,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n              return output, updates\n          else:\n              return self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the shared-neuron GRU network.\"\"\"\n      return GRUWrapper(hparams)\n```\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Aethel I Learned Per-Neuron Embedding MLP\"\ntags: \"embedding, neuron-characteristics, mlp, shared-weights,\
    \ baseline-enhancement, neural-forecasting\"\nabstract: \"This submission enhances the Shared-Neuron MLP baseline by introducing a learnable embedding vector for each of the 71,721 neurons. This neuron-specific embedding is concatenated with each neuron's 4-timestep input, providing unique contextual information before being processed by a shared MLP. This aims to allow the model to implicitly specialize its processing based on learned neuron characteristics.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Learned Per-Neuron Embedding MLP.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'embedding_dim': 16,     # Dimension of each neuron's embedding\n          'mlp_hidden_size': 64,   # Hidden size for the shared MLP\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  class LearnedEmbeddingMLP(nn.Module):\n\
    \      \"\"\"\n      Shared-neuron MLP enhanced with learned per-neuron embeddings.\n      Each neuron's 4-timestep input is augmented with a unique, learned embedding.\n      \"\"\"\n      embedding_dim: int\n      mlp_hidden_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons = x.shape\n\n          # 1. Create and retrieve neuron embeddings\n          # Neuron indices for embedding lookup\n          neuron_indices = jnp.arange(num_neurons) # (num_neurons,)\n\n          # Learnable embedding table: (num_neurons, embedding_dim)\n          neuron_embeddings_table = nn.Embed(\n              num_embeddings=num_neurons,\n              features=self.embedding_dim\n          )(neuron_indices) # (num_neurons, embedding_dim)\n\n          # Expand neuron embeddings to match the batch and input_horizon dimensions of x\n          # Resulting shape: (batch_size,\
    \ input_horizon, num_neurons, embedding_dim)\n          expanded_embeddings = jnp.tile(neuron_embeddings_table[None, None, :, :], (batch_size, input_horizon, 1, 1))\n\n          # 2. Expand original input x to (batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons, 1)\n          x_expanded = x[..., None] # Add a feature dimension of size 1\n\n          # 3. Concatenate expanded x with expanded_embeddings\n          # Resulting shape: (batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons, 1 + embedding_dim)\n          x_combined_features = jnp.concatenate([x_expanded, expanded_embeddings], axis=-1)\n\n          # 4. Reshape for shared MLP\n          # From (batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons, 1 + embedding_dim)\n          # to (batch_size * num_neurons, input_horizon * (1 + embedding_dim))\n          input_dim_mlp = input_horizon * (1 + self.embedding_dim)\n          x_reshaped_for_mlp = jnp.reshape(\n              x_combined_features,\n              (batch_size * num_neurons, input_dim_mlp)\n          )\n\n\
    \          # 5. Apply shared MLP for temporal forecasting\n          # Layer 1 with BatchNorm and Dropout\n          mlp_output = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_size)(x_reshaped_for_mlp)\n          mlp_output = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(mlp_output)\n          mlp_output = nn.relu(mlp_output)\n          mlp_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(mlp_output)\n\n          # Output layer\n          mlp_output = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(mlp_output)\n\n          # Reshape back to (batch_size, output_horizon, num_neurons)\n          # From (batch_size * num_neurons, output_horizon) to (batch_size, num_neurons, output_horizon)\n          final_output = jnp.reshape(mlp_output, (batch_size, num_neurons, self.output_horizon))\n          # Transpose to (batch_size, 32, num_neurons)\n          final_output = jnp.transpose(final_output, (0, 2, 1))\n\n          return final_output\n\n\n  class LearnedEmbeddingMLPWrapper:\n      \"\"\"\n      Wrapper\
    \ to make Flax model compatible with the training system.\n      Handles BatchNorm state and Dropout RNG requirements.\n      \"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = LearnedEmbeddingMLP(\n              embedding_dim=hparams['embedding_dim'],\n              mlp_hidden_size=hparams['mlp_hidden_size'],\n              dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate']\n          )\n          self.mutable = ['batch_stats']\n          self.needs_rng = True\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.model.init(\n              {'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout},\n              dummy_input,\n              training=True\n          )\n          return variables\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if mutable is not None:\n              output, updates = self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n      \
    \            training=training,\n                  mutable=mutable,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n              return output, updates\n          else:\n              return self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the learned embedding enhanced shared-neuron MLP.\"\"\"\n      return LearnedEmbeddingMLPWrapper(hparams)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Today's Research Plan: Fine-tuning and Submitting a New Model**\n\nOkay, so last turn was a bit of a fumble – I tried to save code in the wrong room.  No worries, I'm back in the Research Counter and ready to proceed. My current mission:  first, *correctly* save that debugged GRU code I've been working on to lineage storage.  Then, the main event: implement and submit this \"Learned Per-Neuron Embedding\" model (Research Task 1).\n\nHere's the sequence:\n\n1.  **Save the GRU code:**  `storage write` in the Research Counter.  Simple enough.\n2.  **The \"Learned Per-Neuron Embedding\" Model:** This is where the real work begins.  I'll need to:\n    *   Define the hyperparameters like learning rate, embedding dimension, hidden size for the MLP (if applicable), and dropout rate.\n    *   Create the `LearnedEmbeddingMLP` class. This is the heart of the model.  It'll feature an `nn.Embed` layer to learn embedding vectors for each neuron.  The trick is to handle these neuron\
  \ indices (0 to 71720) correctly.  I'll concatenate the learned embedding with the input data (which is the previous 4 time steps) for each neuron, feeding that combined input into a shared MLP.\n    *   I'll then build a `LearnedEmbeddingMLPWrapper` to integrate this with the system.\n    *   Once that's done, I'll submit it for Research Task 1.\n\n**Diving Deeper into the Embedding Implementation**\n\nOkay, let's nail down the specifics of this embedding-based model.\n\n*   **Architecture:**\n    *   I'll define hyperparameters within the `_define_hyperparameters` to include an `embedding_dim` (let's start with 16 or 32) and potentially `mlp_hidden_size` for the MLP that comes after the embeddings (similar to my ID 571 success, which might inform whether or not I want a deeper MLP).\n    *   Inside the model's `__call__` method, I'll need to:\n        *   Get the `num_neurons` in the input.\n        *   Create neuron indices using `jnp.arange(num_neurons)`.\n        *   Construct the\
  \ embeddings by calling `nn.Embed` on the indices. `nn.Embed(num_embeddings=num_neurons, features=self.embedding_dim)(jnp.arange(num_neurons))` will yield a table of embeddings.\n        *   Crucially, I'll need to broadcast the embedding across the batch size and time dimensions.\n            *   I can tile the embedding to `(batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons, embedding_dim)` by reshaping it to `(1, 1, num_neurons, embedding_dim)` and tiling it.\n            *   I'll ensure the input `x` has a feature dimension of 1, resulting in shape `(batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons, 1)`.\n        *   I'll concatenate the input and embeddings along the last axis to get `(batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons, 1 + embedding_dim)`.\n        *   Then I'll reshape this to `(batch_size * num_neurons, input_horizon * (1 + embedding_dim))` for the shared MLP.\n        *   The MLP itself will either be a basic single-hidden-layer structure (like my original baseline) or maybe a deeper one if `hidden_size_mlp`\
  \ is provided, again informed by the previous success.  I'll likely start with the baseline to isolate the effect of the embeddings.\n*   **Key Consideration: Neuron Order** The neuron order is fixed, so I can fetch the embeddings efficiently. I should define the embedding layer inside `nn.compact` to make sure it functions as expected.\n*   **nn.Embed input:** `nn.Embed` takes a batch of indices.  Since I need embeddings for all neurons, `nn.Embed(num_embeddings=num_neurons, features=embedding_dim)` will create `(71721, embedding_dim)`. I then generate those indices `jnp.arange(num_neurons)`, pass those to `nn.Embed` to fetch the embedding values.\n    *   Let's create the indices directly. `jnp.arange(num_neurons)` will yield `(num_neurons,)`.\n    *   `nn.Embed(num_embeddings=num_neurons, features=embedding_dim)(jnp.arange"
