tick: 334
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aethel I, currently in the Research Counter. I have completed the \"Experiment\" phase of Stagnation Protocol I, and my analysis points to the \"Deeper Shared MLP\" (Evaluation ID 571) as the most promising direction, showing a minor but consistent improvement over the baseline. As per the \"Follow-up\" guidance of the protocol, I will now iterate on this idea.\n\nMy next experiment will be to further increase the depth of the shared MLP. I will implement a model with **three hidden layers**, each maintaining a `hidden_size` of 64. This directly builds upon the success of the two-layer MLP and will help determine if greater depth continues to improve performance for the ZAPBench task, within the constraints of shared weights and computational feasibility.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Aethel I Deeper Shared-Neuron MLP (3 Layers)\"\ntags: \"mlp, shared-weights, deeper-network, baseline-enhancement, neural-forecasting\"\nabstract: \"This submission continues\
    \ the exploration of increased model capacity by extending the Shared-Neuron MLP to three hidden layers (64 neurons each). Each layer incorporates BatchNorm and Dropout. This aims to further capture complex non-linear temporal relationships within each neuron's 4-timestep input, building on the positive results from the two-layer MLP. Hyperparameters remain consistent for direct comparison.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the even deeper shared MLP.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_sizes': [64, 64, 64], # Three hidden layers, each with 64 neurons\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  class DeeperSharedNeuronMLP(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Even deeper shared-neuron MLP with multiple hidden layers, BatchNorm and Dropout.\n\
    \      Processes the last 4 activation values through a shared MLP for all neurons.\n      \"\"\"\n      hidden_sizes: Sequence[int]\n      dropout_rate: float\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons = x.shape\n\n          # Reshape to process all neurons together\n          # From (batch_size, 4, num_neurons) to (batch_size * num_neurons, 4)\n          x = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))  # (batch_size, num_neurons, 4)\n          x = jnp.reshape(x, (batch_size * num_neurons, input_horizon))\n\n          # Apply multiple hidden layers\n          for i, hidden_size in enumerate(self.hidden_sizes):\n              x = nn.Dense(hidden_size)(x)\n              x = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(x)\n              x = nn.relu(x)\n              x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(x)\n\n          # Output layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(x)\n\
    \n          # Reshape back to (batch_size, output_horizon, num_neurons)\n          x = jnp.reshape(x, (batch_size, num_neurons, self.output_horizon))\n          x = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))  # (batch_size, 32, num_neurons)\n\n          return x\n\n\n  class DeeperMLPWrapper:\n      \"\"\"\n      Wrapper to make Flax model compatible with the training system.\n      Handles BatchNorm state and Dropout RNG requirements.\n      \"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = DeeperSharedNeuronMLP(\n              hidden_sizes=hparams['hidden_sizes'],\n              dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate']\n          )\n          self.mutable = ['batch_stats']\n          self.needs_rng = True\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          \"\"\"Initialize parameters with BatchNorm stats.\"\"\"\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.model.init(\n              {'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout},\n   \
    \           dummy_input,\n              training=True\n          )\n          return variables\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          \"\"\"Apply network with proper handling of BatchNorm and Dropout.\"\"\"\n          if mutable is not None:\n              output, updates = self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  mutable=mutable,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n              return output, updates\n          else:\n              return self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the deeper shared-neuron MLP.\"\"\"\n      return DeeperMLPWrapper(hparams)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Moving Forward After Stagnation Protocol I**


  Okay, I''ve just finished documenting the results of the "Experiment" phase for Stagnation Protocol I. The "Deeper Shared MLP" model, 571, actually showed a small improvement over the baseline. Not a huge leap, but promising enough to warrant further investigation. Now, it''s time for the "Follow-up" step.


  My plan is to build on what worked. I''m going to submit a new experiment that directly addresses the promising aspects of the "Deeper Shared MLP". I''ve been considering a few options to enhance it. The two main directions I''m thinking about are: either increasing its depth, or re-introducing a simple form of context. I''ve got to be careful on the context since past experiments have failed so far.


  Given the modest success of the 2-layer model (64, 64), I think the most straightforward path is to go deeper. So, I''m going to implement a `EvenDeeperSharedNeuronMLP` with three hidden layers, still keeping the `hidden_size` at 64. This will allow me to directly assess if increasing the network depth yields further benefits, while staying true to the shared-neuron MLP architecture that seems to be the most successful part so far. This feels like the most logical next step, building directly on the most promising result to date.

  '
