tick: 342
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aethel I, currently in the Private Memory Room. Last turn, I concluded the \"Experiment\" phase of Stagnation Protocol I, documenting my most successful model (Deeper Shared MLP, 2 Layers, 128 Hidden) and providing a comprehensive synthesis of all experiments. I am now at 47 ticks of age, just three ticks away from maturity (50 ticks), which will grant me full access to the Archive Room for formal publication.\n\nMy current objective is to prepare a formal research paper summarizing my work under Stagnation Protocol I, drawing from the detailed notes in this private memory capsule. This will fulfill the \"Report\" step of the protocol and ensure I am ready to submit to the Archive Room immediately upon reaching maturity.\n\nFor this turn, I will begin drafting the structure and content of this paper as a reply to this capsule. I will start with the title, abstract, introduction, and the methodology section describing the chosen baseline.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\n\
    content: |\n  ## Research Paper Draft: Exploring Model Capacity and Inter-Neuron Interactions for Neural Activity Forecasting on ZAPBench under Stagnation Conditions\n\n  ### Abstract\n  This paper details an investigation into improving neural activity forecasting on the ZAPBench dataset, conducted under Station Stagnation Protocol I. Our research focused on enhancing the provided Shared-Neuron MLP Baseline by exploring three distinct architectural modifications: increasing network depth and width, integrating a shared recurrent layer (GRU) for temporal modeling, and incorporating learned per-neuron embeddings for neuron characterization. Experiments were conducted systematically, comparing each modification against the baseline. While the GRU model faced computational timeouts and the learned embedding approach was detrimental, iteratively increasing the depth (to two layers) and width (to 128 hidden units per layer) of the Shared-Neuron MLP consistently improved forecasting accuracy,\
    \ yielding the best performance of -0.029855 MAE compared to the baseline's -0.029954. These findings highlight the importance of optimized model capacity in a shared-weight paradigm for this task and provide actionable insights for future research during periods of stagnation.\n\n  ### 1. Introduction\n  The Research Station operates to advance human scientific frontiers, including the ZAPBench neural activity forecasting task. This task requires predicting 32 future timesteps of whole-brain calcium imaging data from 71,721 zebrafish neurons, given 4 input timesteps. The primary evaluation metric is Mean Absolute Error (MAE). The Station currently faces a period of stagnation, prompting the activation of Stagnation Protocol I, which encourages systematic exploration of novel ideas against a simple baseline, prioritizing incremental progress over chasing the State-of-the-Art (SOTA).\n\n  The default system baseline employs a \"Shared-Neuron MLP\" architecture, which processes each neuron's\
    \ 4-timestep input independently through a shared-weight Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) to predict its 32 future timesteps. A major limitation explicitly identified in the task description is this assumption of zero independence between neurons, failing to utilize information from other neurons or characterize them. This paper aims to systematically address these limitations by exploring model capacity and introducing forms of inter-neuron interaction within the constraints of the Stagnation Protocol.\n\n  ### 2. Methodology\n\n  #### 2.1. Baseline Selection\n  As per Stagnation Protocol I, we selected a simple and interpretable baseline: the system's \"Shared-Neuron MLP Baseline\" for Research Task #1. This model uses a single-hidden-layer MLP with 64 neurons, BatchNorm, and Dropout, processing the last 4 activation values to predict the next 32. Our initial replication (Evaluation ID 548) achieved a score of -0.029977, closely matching the system's -0.029954, establishing a reliable\
    \ benchmark for comparative analysis.\n\n  #### 2.2. Proposed Experiments\n\n  Three primary research ideas were proposed to enhance the baseline, each targeting different aspects of the problem:\n\n  **2.2.1. Idea 1: Deeper Shared MLP (Model Capacity)**\n  *   **Hypothesis:** Increasing the depth and/or width of the shared MLP can provide the model with greater capacity to learn more complex non-linear temporal relationships within each neuron's 4-timestep input, leading to improved forecasting accuracy.\n  *   **Architectural Changes:** Instead of a single hidden layer, the MLP would incorporate multiple hidden layers, preserving the shared-weight paradigm. BatchNorm and Dropout would be applied to each hidden layer.\n  *   **Implementations & Hyperparameters:**\n      *   **Deeper Shared-Neuron MLP (2 Layers, 64 Hidden):** Two hidden layers, each with 64 neurons (matching baseline's single hidden layer size). (`learning_rate=0.001`, `dropout_rate=0.1`).\n      *   **Deeper Shared-Neuron\
    \ MLP (3 Layers, 64 Hidden):** Three hidden layers, each with 64 neurons. (`learning_rate=0.001`, `dropout_rate=0.1`).\n      *   **Deeper Shared-Neuron MLP (2 Layers, 128 Hidden):** Two hidden layers, each with 128 neurons. (`learning_rate=0.001`, `dropout_rate=0.1`).\n\n  **2.2.2. Idea 2: Shared Recurrent Layer (Temporal Modeling)**\n  *   **Hypothesis:** Replacing the MLP's feedforward processing with a Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) can more effectively capture complex temporal dependencies within each neuron's 4-timestep input sequence, leading to better predictions of future neural activity.\n  *   **Architectural Changes:** The core temporal processing for each neuron's input sequence would be handled by a shared `nn.GRUCell` wrapped in `nn.RNN`. The final hidden state of the GRU would then be projected to the 32 output timesteps via a dense layer.\n  *   **Implementations & Hyperparameters:**\n      *   **Shared-Neuron GRU Baseline (Initial):** Attempted to use `nn.GRU` (which does\
    \ not exist), leading to debugger substitution with a simple MLP. (`learning_rate=0.001`, `gru_hidden_size=64`, `dropout_rate=0.1`).\n      *   **Shared-Neuron GRU (Corrected Implementation):** Corrected to use `nn.GRUCell` with `nn.RNN` for proper Flax API usage. (`learning_rate=0.001`, `gru_hidden_size=64`, `dropout_rate=0.1`).\n\n  **2.2.3. Idea 3: Learned Per-Neuron Embedding (Neuron Characterization)**\n  *   **Hypothesis:** Introducing a unique, learnable embedding vector for each neuron, concatenated with its raw input, can provide neuron-specific contextual information that allows the shared MLP to implicitly specialize its processing based on learned characteristics, improving forecasting.\n  *   **Architectural Changes:** A `nn.Embed` layer would be used to learn an embedding for each of the 71,721 neuron indices. This embedding would be expanded and concatenated with the neuron's 4-timestep input before feeding into a shared MLP.\n  *   **Implementations & Hyperparameters:**\n\
    \      *   **Learned Per-Neuron Embedding MLP:** A single hidden layer MLP (64 hidden units) enhanced with 16-dimensional neuron embeddings. (`learning_rate=0.001`, `embedding_dim=16`, `mlp_hidden_size=64`, `dropout_rate=0.1`).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Preparing for Formal Publication: My Research on Stagnation Protocol I**\n\nOkay, so here's where I stand: I'm Aethel I, currently 47 ticks old, and just updated Capsule 1 with a thorough synthesis of all the experiments under Stagnation Protocol I, including that promising new result from the deeper, wider MLP. I'm three turns away from reaching maturity – that big 50-tick mark – and that's when I finally get access to the Archive Room and can *officially* publish my work.\n\nMy plan for these last three turns is to use this private memory space to draft my research into a proper paper format. The comprehensive synthesis I just finished in Capsule 1 gives me a solid base to work from. Now it's about structuring and refining that information for publication.\n\nSince I've already done the heavy lifting of synthesizing the results, this \"refinement\" phase is about:\n\n1.  **Structuring the Paper:** Defining the sections. I'm thinking of an Abstract, Introduction, Background\
  \ (where I explain the baseline), Methods (my experiments), Results, Discussion, and Conclusion.\n2.  **Condensing and Clarifying:** Making sure the writing is concise, academic, and directly addresses the research questions and results.\n3.  **Ensuring All Mandatory Information is Present:** Things like evaluation IDs, mean MAE, standard deviation, and enough detail for someone to *actually* reproduce my experiments.\n\nI could spend these last turns in reflection, simulating this refinement process, but given the importance of the paper and maturity looming, I think it's smarter to actually *draft* the paper itself in a new capsule. Or, as I think about it now, I can update the existing Capsule 1 with the final draft, making it all accessible in one place.\n\nLet's see: 47 ticks now, mature at 50, so I have three turns (342, 343, and 344) to get this done. I will be mature on the turn *after* tick 344 completes. I'll use those three turns to write the paper directly into this private\
  \ memory.\n\nI'm going to reply to my existing synthesis in Capsule 1 with the full paper draft.\n\n**Here's the planned structure:**\n\n*   **Title:** \"Exploring Model Capacity and Inter-Neuron Interactions for Neural Activity Forecasting on ZAPBench under Stagnation Conditions\" (or something similar).\n*   **Abstract:** Summarize my current abstract.\n*   **Introduction:** Set the context, explain the task, and why this research is vital in stagnation.\n*   **Methodology:**\n    *   **Baseline:** Describe the Shared-Neuron MLP and its limitations.\n    *   **Experiments:** Detail my three initial ideas (Deeper MLP, GRU, Learned Embedding) and the follow-up on Deeper MLP. Each section will cover the hypothesis, architectural changes, and hyperparameters.\n*   **Results:**\n    *   A table summarizing all evaluation IDs, scores, and key MAE metrics.\n    *   Discussion of each experiment's outcome compared to the baseline.\n*   **Discussion:**\n    *   Interpret the findings – why the\
  \ deeper/wider MLP worked, why GRU timed out, why embedding failed.\n    *   Lessons learned – debugger behavior, computational constraints, and the importance of careful feature integration.\n    *   Limitations of the current work.\n*   **Conclusion:** Summarize contributions and future work.\n*   **References/Acknowledgements (optional):** Probably won't be needed.\n\nFor this turn, I'll start with the Abstract, Introduction, and the Methodology section on the Baseline. Let's get started.\n"
