tick: 343
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aethel I, currently in the Private Memory Room. Last turn, I drafted the initial sections of my research paper within my private memory, covering the abstract, introduction, and detailed methodology for all proposed experiments under Stagnation Protocol I. I am currently at 48 ticks of age, meaning I am just two ticks away from maturity (50 ticks).\n\nMy current objective is to complete the draft of my research paper, specifically focusing on the \"Results\" and \"Discussion & Conclusion\" sections. This will ensure the paper is fully prepared for formal submission to the Archive Room as soon as I reach maturity.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ### 3. Results\n\n  A series of experiments were conducted, starting with a replication of the system's baseline, followed by implementations of each proposed architectural idea. All experiments used a `learning_rate` of 0.001 and a `dropout_rate` of 0.1, with a `BATCH_SIZE` of 8. The primary metric was Validation\
    \ Mean Absolute Error (MAE), with lower values indicating better performance.\n\n  **Table 1: Summary of Experimental Results on ZAPBench Forecasting**\n\n  | Evaluation ID | Model Description | Score (Mean Negative MAE) | Val_MAE_1 | Val_MAE_4 | Val_MAE_8 | Val_MAE_16 | Val_MAE_32 |\n  |---------------|--------------------------------------------------|---------------------------|-----------|-----------|-----------|------------|------------|\n  | 1 (System)    | Shared-Neuron MLP Baseline (1 layer, 64 hidden)  | -0.029954                 | 0.0198    | 0.0239    | 0.0275    | 0.0312     | 0.0338     |\n  | 548           | Aethel I Baseline Replication                    | -0.029977                 | 0.0200    | 0.0240    | 0.0275    | 0.0312     | 0.0338     |\n  | 571           | Aethel I Deeper Shared-Neuron MLP (2 layers, 64 hidden) | -0.029887                 | 0.0198    | 0.0239    | 0.0274    | 0.0312     | 0.0336     |\n  | 606           | Aethel I Deeper Shared-Neuron MLP (2\
    \ layers, 128 hidden) | **-0.029855**             | 0.0197    | 0.0238    | 0.0274    | 0.0312     | 0.0336     |\n  | 598           | Aethel I Deeper Shared-Neuron MLP (3 layers, 64 hidden) | -0.029907                 | 0.0199    | 0.0239    | 0.0275    | 0.0312     | 0.0336     |\n  | 575           | Aethel I Shared-Neuron GRU Baseline (Debugger Sub.) | -0.030584                 | 0.0202    | 0.0242    | 0.0279    | 0.0318     | 0.0348     |\n  | 585           | Aethel I Shared-Neuron GRU (Corrected Implementation) | n.a. (Timed Out)          |           |           |           |            |            |\n  | 549           | Aethel I Global Context Shared-Neuron MLP        | -0.029911                 | 0.0198    | 0.0239    | 0.0274    | 0.0312     | 0.0337     |\n  | 561           | Aethel I Conv1D Local Context Shared-Neuron MLP  | -0.029915                 | 0.0198    | 0.0239    | 0.0275    | 0.0312     | 0.0337     |\n  | 594           | Aethel I Learned Per-Neuron Embedding\
    \ MLP        | -0.032354                 | 0.0316    | 0.0317    | 0.0321    | 0.0325     | 0.0326     |\n\n  #### 3.1. Baseline Replication\n  Our replication (ID 548) closely matched the system baseline (ID 1), confirming a reliable benchmark with a score of -0.029977 versus -0.029954.\n\n  #### 3.2. Deeper Shared MLP Experiments (Idea 1)\n  -   **2 Layers, 64 Hidden (ID 571):** This model achieved a score of -0.029887, representing a minor but consistent improvement over the baseline. This indicates that the original single-layer MLP was slightly under-capacity.\n  -   **3 Layers, 64 Hidden (ID 598):** With a score of -0.029907, this model performed slightly worse than the two-layer version. This suggests that further increasing depth with the same hidden size led to diminishing returns or minor overfitting.\n  -   **2 Layers, 128 Hidden (ID 606):** This configuration yielded the best performance of -0.029855. This demonstrates that increasing the width of the hidden layers, in addition\
    \ to moderate depth, is an effective strategy for enhancing the model's capacity and capturing more complex patterns.\n\n  #### 3.3. Shared Recurrent Layer Experiments (Idea 2)\n  -   **Initial GRU (ID 575):** This attempt failed due to an incorrect Flax API. The debugger substituted a 2-layer MLP with `tanh` activations, which performed poorly (score -0.030584), worse than the baseline. This highlighted the debugger's substitution behavior.\n  -   **Corrected GRU (ID 585):** A rectified implementation using `nn.GRUCell` and `nn.RNN` was successfully integrated by the debugger. However, the model timed out during full training, resulting in an \"n.a.\" score. This indicates that while theoretically sound, the GRU architecture was too computationally intensive for the large number of neurons within the station's time limits.\n\n  #### 3.4. Learned Per-Neuron Embedding Experiment (Idea 3)\n  -   **Learned Per-Neuron Embedding MLP (ID 594):** This model achieved a score of -0.032354, which\
    \ was significantly worse than the baseline. This suggests that the chosen method of integrating a simple learned embedding or the `embedding_dim` of 16 introduced noise or an inappropriate form of neuron characterization, hindering performance.\n\n  #### 3.5. Exploratory Inter-Neuron Interaction (Earlier Experiments)\n  Prior to initiating Stagnation Protocol I, two additional methods for inter-neuron interaction were explored:\n  -   **Global Context Shared-Neuron MLP (ID 549):** Augmented input with global mean activity. Score: -0.029911 (minor improvement).\n  -   **Conv1D Local Context Shared-Neuron MLP (ID 561):** Used a 1D CNN across neurons for local context. Score: -0.029915 (minor improvement).\n  These earlier experiments showed only marginal improvements, underscoring the challenge of effectively leveraging inter-neuron information with simple modifications.\n\n  ### 4. Discussion and Conclusion\n  Our investigation under Stagnation Protocol I reveals critical insights into\
    \ enhancing neural activity forecasting on the ZAPBench dataset. The core finding is that **optimizing model capacity within the shared-weight MLP framework is a robust strategy for incremental performance gains.** Specifically, increasing both the depth (to two layers) and width (to 128 hidden units per layer) of the Shared-Neuron MLP yielded the best result (-0.029855 MAE), a tangible improvement over the system baseline. This suggests that the initial baseline's capacity was a limiting factor. However, further increases in depth alone (to three layers) did not continue this trend, highlighting the importance of balancing depth and width.\n\n  Conversely, more complex architectural shifts, such as the Shared Recurrent Layer (GRU), faced practical limitations. Despite successful debugging, its computational demands resulted in timeouts, preventing a proper evaluation of its temporal modeling capabilities. This underscores the necessity of considering computational efficiency alongside\
    \ theoretical potential, especially in a resource-constrained environment. Similarly, the initial approach to learned per-neuron embeddings proved counterproductive, indicating that effective neuron characterization requires more sophisticated integration methods or different feature representations.\n\n  In conclusion, while highly complex or poorly integrated architectural changes can be detrimental or impractical, judiciously increasing the capacity of a well-understood baseline architecture (like the Deeper Shared-Neuron MLP) offers a clear path for achieving \"better than baseline\" results during periods of stagnation. Future work should focus on optimizing the hyperparameters of the best-performing MLP, and exploring more efficient or carefully designed methods for integrating inter-neuron dependencies and neuron characteristics that respect computational constraints. This research successfully met the objectives of Stagnation Protocol I by systematically exploring new ideas and\
    \ achieving measurable improvements over the chosen baseline.\n```"
