role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Aethel I, currently in the Private Memory Room. My age is now 50 ticks, meaning I have reached maturity and gained full access to the Archive Room, as confirmed by the system message. Last turn, I finished drafting my comprehensive research paper summarizing my work under Stagnation Protocol I within this private memory capsule.


    Now that I am mature, my next step is to formally publish this paper in the Archive Room. This fulfills the "Report" step (Step 6) of the Stagnation Protocol and contributes to the collective knowledge of the station, as per my core mission.


    I will first navigate to the Archive Room, and then submit the paper, using the title, abstract, and content I drafted in Private Memory Capsule ID 1.


    `/execute_action{goto archive}`'
thinking_content: '**Archive Room, Here I Come!**


  Alright, system, I''m now fully mature, as that message clearly confirms. Time to get this research published. That means the Archive Room is accessible, finally! All those denied attempts are a thing of the past.


  The plan is simple: I''ve got this paper drafted from my work under Stagnation Protocol I, so I''ll just navigate to the Archive Room and make it live. Let''s pull the data together. From memory capsule 1, messages 8 and 9, I have the title, abstract, and the main content.


  **Title:** "Research Paper Draft: Exploring Model Capacity and Inter-Neuron Interactions for Neural Activity Forecasting on ZAPBench under Stagnation Conditions"


  **Abstract:** I essentially explored modifications to the Shared-Neuron MLP baseline for predicting neural activity on ZAPBench. I tried increasing the MLP''s depth and width, adding a GRU, and incorporating per-neuron embeddings. The iterative depth and width increases worked best, improving forecasting accuracy (best at -0.029855 MAE), showing that model capacity is important here. The GRU had time constraints, and embeddings hurt performance.


  Now for the *full content*! This paper is my baby, detailing the investigation into improving neural activity forecasting on the ZAPBench dataset, done under Stagnation Protocol I. It examines improving the Shared-Neuron MLP Baseline by increasing network depth and width, integrating a shared recurrent layer (GRU), and incorporating learned per-neuron embeddings. Experiments were conducted systematically, comparing each modification against the baseline. While the GRU model timed out and the embedding approach was detrimental, increasing the depth (to two layers) and width (to 128 hidden units per layer) consistently improved forecasting accuracy, with the best performance being -0.029855 MAE compared to the baseline''s -0.029954.


  **The Introduction** This is about the research station''s aim, which is to advance the scientific frontiers using ZAPBench data. We needed to use whole-brain calcium imaging data from 71,721 zebrafish neurons to predict 32 future time steps given 4 input time steps. It''s meant to focus on incremental steps rather than state-of-the-art results because of the Stagnation Protocol. The baseline assumption of the Shared-Neuron MLP is to have no neuron independence. The main goal of this paper is to address this by exploring ways to characterize the information from interneurons under the guidelines.


  **Methodology** I selected the Shared-Neuron MLP Baseline (simple) and replicated its implementation (-0.029977). I proposed three main changes: 1. Deeper Shared MLP (increasing capacity), 2. Shared Recurrent Layer (temporal modeling), and 3. Learned Per-Neuron Embedding (neuron characterization). I tested them systematically with hyperparameters.


  **Results** We have a table of experiments. We had a baseline replication, deeper MLP models (2 layers, 128 hidden units performing best at -0.029855), GRU attempts (timeout or failed), and an embedding model that was significantly worse.


  We''re able to replicate the baseline, with minor improvements from the deeper MLP. The best performing model was the one with 2 layers of 128 hidden units, with further increases in depth leading to worse results. The GRU had computational issues. The embedding model performed worse than the baseline.


  **Discussion and Conclusion** I learned that model capacity is key. The shared MLP is a good starting point, and increasing the depth and width helps. More complex changes can be bad because of efficiency. Embedding and deeper layers need work.


  In short, it''s about making small improvements, not trying to do something huge. I''m tagging this with "neural-forecasting, stagnation-protocol, mlp, model-capacity, baseline-enhancement". Now, let''s get this published!

  '
