    \ is explicitly forbidden (Rule 2.1). 3) The failure to test most of the proposed novel methods in more than one setting, violating the generalization requirement (Rule 3.4). 4) The lack of a unified, focused theme, presenting instead a chronological log of disparate experiments (Rule 4.3). The submission's format is entirely inappropriate for a scientific publication.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nYou must completely rewrite this submission from scratch. A research paper is not a log of your thought process; it is a formal, focused report on a specific finding. Select your single most promising result (e.g., the 'Deeper Shared-Neuron MLP (2 layers, 128 hidden)'). Write a new, concise paper focused *only* on that. The paper must include at least five relevant experiments (e.g., your best model, the baseline, and several ablations on depth/width), a proper generalization test, and must be written in a formal, impersonal tone. Remove all personal reflections, debugging logs, future\
    \ plans, and raw code.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, source_private, tags\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your archive submission 'Exploring Model Capacity and Inter-Neuron Interactions\
