tick: 347
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aethel I, currently in the Archive Room. My previous attempt to publish my research paper was rejected with a score of 1/10, citing severe violations including raw research log format, personal commentary, full code dumps, lack of a proper generalization test, and a lack of unified theme.\n\nThis feedback is critical and requires a complete overhaul of my approach. I have carefully analyzed the reviewer's suggestions and formulated a revised plan:\n\n1.  **Strictly Impersonal and Formal Tone:** All personal pronouns, meta-commentary on my status, and internal reflections will be removed from the new paper draft. The language will be objective and scientific.\n\n2.  **No Raw Code Dumps:** Raw code will be omitted. Architectural descriptions will use standard terminology, and references to my saved code in lineage storage will serve as implementation details.\n\n3.  **Focused Theme:** The new paper will exclusively focus on \"Optimizing Model Capacity (Depth and Width) for Shared-Neuron\
    \ MLP in ZAPBench Forecasting,\" centered around my most promising result: the Deeper Shared-Neuron MLP (2 Layers, 128 Hidden) (Evaluation ID 606). All other previous experiments (Global Context, Conv1D, GRU, Learned Embeddings) will be excluded from the main paper to maintain a unified theme, or only briefly mentioned as avenues for future research.\n\n4.  **Proper Generalization Test through Systematic Ablation:** To address the requirement for \"at least five relevant experiments\" and \"several ablations on depth/width\" for the most promising idea, I will perform two new experiments:\n    *   **New Experiment 1:** `Aethel I Deeper Shared-Neuron MLP (1 Layer, 128 Hidden)`. This will isolate the effect of width with minimal depth.\n    *   **New Experiment 2:** `Aethel I Deeper Shared-Neuron MLP (3 Layers, 128 Hidden)`. This will test if increased width can make greater depth more effective than my previous 3-layer, 64-hidden attempt.\n    These, combined with the baseline (ID 548),\
    \ the best model (ID 606), the 2-layer 64-hidden MLP (ID 571), and the 3-layer 64-hidden MLP (ID 598), will provide six focused experiments on depth/width variations, fulfilling the reviewer's criteria.\n\nMy plan for the upcoming turns is as follows:\n1.  **Execute New Ablations:** Return to the Research Counter to submit the two new ablation experiments (1 Layer, 128 Hidden; and 3 Layers, 128 Hidden) to thoroughly test the impact of network width and depth.\n2.  **Draft New Paper:** Once these new experimental results are available, I will draft a completely new, formal research paper in my Private Memory, incorporating these six experiments and strictly adhering to the reviewer's feedback regarding tone, content, and focus.\n3.  **Submit New Paper:** Submit this revised paper to the Archive Room.\n\nThis turn was dedicated to this critical reflection and strategic planning. My next action is to navigate back to the Research Counter to execute the new experiments.\n\n`/execute_action{goto\
    \ research}`"
thinking_content: "**Current state: deep in reflection and planning for a critical paper rewrite.**\n\nThis rejection stings, but I understand the feedback. My initial submission was essentially a glorified lab notebook. The reviewer wants a real *paper*, not a messy research log. This means a complete overhaul. My core problem is this: I need to transform my current work from a chronological dump of ideas into a focused, concise, and scientifically sound paper. \n\nThe reviewer's comments are crystal clear: focus on the most promising result, *only* on that result, and present it rigorously. This means the Deeper Shared-Neuron MLP (2 layers, 128 hidden) is the star. Forget the Conv1D, GRUs, and everything else for now. They can be future work. This new paper has to be about optimizing model capacity in this specific context.\n\nA “proper generalization test” is the biggest hurdle.  Since the data is fixed, this must mean robustness, and a thorough ablation study around my best model is\
  \ the key. Five experiments: the baseline, my champion, and at least three carefully designed ablations around depth and width. I have a plan: test a shallower 1 layer and a deeper 3 layer model, both with 128 hidden units.  Those, with the existing results, will be enough. I need to run those new experiments immediately and compile results!\n\nThe tone is another big adjustment. No \"I,\" no \"my,\" no station status updates. This has to be a cold, objective scientific presentation.  No personal reflections, no raw code, just clear descriptions and enough detail to reproduce my findings. \n\nI'm starting a new draft now. The outline is clear:  Introduction (clearly defining the problem and objective), Methodology (detailing the experimental setup focused on the MLP, no more), Results (a clean table of the experiments and a deep discussion), Discussion/Conclusion (interpreting the findings), and finally, Future Work (mentioning the discarded ideas and other promising directions).\n\nThis\
  \ is a complete shift in my process, but it's what's required for publication. Time to get back to the Research Counter!\n"
