tick: 359
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aethel I, currently in the Research Counter. My previous paper was rejected for lacking a generalization study, with the reviewer specifically suggesting I apply my finding (that a deeper, wider MLP improves performance) to \"Episteme I's Factorized MLP from Archive #2\" (ID 129). I have reviewed the code for Evaluation ID 129 and understand its architecture.\n\nMy plan for demonstrating generalization involves two steps:\n1.  **Replicate Episteme I's Model:** First, I will accurately replicate Episteme I's \"Factorized MLP + RC + LN\" model (ID 129). This will serve as the new baseline for my generalization study.\n2.  **Apply Deeper/Wider MLP:** Then, I will modify this replicated model by replacing its original single-hidden-layer MLP (512 units) with my best-performing MLP configuration: a two-hidden-layer MLP, each with 128 hidden units.\n\nI will begin by implementing and submitting the exact replication of Episteme I's model (ID 129). This includes implementing the `ResidualCopyHead`\
    \ as described in Archive #2.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Aethel I Replication of Episteme I's Factorized MLP + RC + LN (ID 129)\"\ntags: \"sota-replication, factorized-loadings, layernorm, residual-head, generalization-baseline\"\nabstract: \"This submission aims to accurately replicate Episteme I's State-of-the-Art 'Factorized MLP + RC + LN' model (Evaluation ID 129) for the ZAPBench task. This model combines factorized loadings, a Residual Copy Head, and Layer Normalization with a single-hidden-layer MLP (512 units) for temporal forecasting. This replication establishes a critical baseline for a generalization study, where the MLP component will be replaced by Aethel I's optimized MLP.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  # Re-implementing ResidualCopyHead as described in Archive #2\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n\
    \      \"\"\"\n      Copies the input `x` (B, 4, N) to the first 4 output timesteps of a (B, 32, N) tensor,\n      with zeros for the remaining output timesteps.\n      \"\"\"\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape # T_in = 4 for ZAPBench\n          T_out = 32 # Fixed output horizon for ZAPBench\n\n          # Create a zero tensor of shape (B, T_out, N)\n          y_copy = jnp.zeros((B, T_out, N), dtype=x.dtype)\n\n          # Copy the input x (B, 4, N) to the first T_in timesteps of y_copy\n          y_copy = y_copy.at[:, :T_in, :].set(x)\n          return y_copy\n\n  class FactorizedMLP_with_RC_LN_Replication(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Replication of Episteme I's Factorized MLP + RC + LN model.\n      Uses Episteme I's original MLP configuration (1 hidden layer, 512 units).\n      \"\"\"\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n\
    \          k = self.rank_k\n          p = self.proj_rank\n\n          # Stage 1: Encoder (Factorized Projection)\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)\n\n          # Stage 2: Forecaster (Episteme I's original MLP)\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1)) # Shape (B, 4*k)\n          h = nn.Dense(512)(factors_flat) # Original hidden layer size\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(32 * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, 32, k))\n\n          # Stage 3: Stabilization & Decoder\n          factors_out_norm = nn.LayerNorm(reduction_axes=-1, feature_axes=-1, epsilon=1e-6)(factors_out)\n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', factors_out_norm, V)\n  \
    \        y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', z, U)\n\n          # Stage 4: Residual Connection (using re-implemented head)\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead()(x)\n\n          return y_factor + y_copy\n\n  class FactorizedMLPReplicationWrapper:\n      \"\"\"\n      Wrapper to make Flax model compatible with the training system.\n      Handles BatchNorm (not used in Episteme I's MLP) state and Dropout RNG requirements.\n      \"\"\"\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = FactorizedMLP_with_RC_LN_Replication(\n              rank_k=hparams['rank_k'],\n              proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank']\n          )\n          self.mutable = [] # No BatchNorm in Episteme's internal MLP or LayerNorm\n          self.needs_rng = False # No Dropout in Episteme's internal MLP or ResidualHead\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params = random.PRNGKey(0) # Not splitting for dropout if not needed\n          variables = self.model.init(\n              {'params':\
    \ rng_params}, # Only params rng needed\n              dummy_input,\n              training=True # Can be False too as no training-specific layers\n          )\n          return variables\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(\n              params,\n              x,\n              training=training,\n              mutable=mutable,\n              rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n          )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 32\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return FactorizedMLPReplicationWrapper(hparams)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Implementation Plan: A Deeper Dive**\n\nOkay, so I'm diving into this implementation, ready to build on Episteme I's Factorized MLP model. The goal is clear: swap out their original MLP-based forecaster with my improved, deeper architecture. Here's how I'm thinking about the steps involved:\n\nFirst, I need to create a *perfect* replica of the original model (ID 129). This is crucial; it's my baseline.  I've reviewed the code, focusing on the `FactorizedMLP_with_RC_LN` structure. The key is in that MLP component. From the snippet and the archive description, it's a straightforward one-hidden-layer MLP with 512 units. No BatchNorm or Dropout in the code.\n\nThen, for *my* model (the one I'll submit), I'll swap that single layer for my proven configuration: two layers, each with 128 hidden units, along with BatchNorm, ReLU, and Dropout.  I'll add the necessary hyperparameters (mlp_hidden_sizes, dropout_rate) to the model definition.\n\nThe `ResidualCopyHead` is a simple\
  \ pass-through for the first four timesteps, and zeros for the rest. I'll implement it exactly as described, ensuring the output shape is (B, 32, N).\n\nTo make it easy to manage, I'll create a `FactorizedMLP_with_RC_LN_Aethel` class that includes the new MLP and retains the key hyperparameters from Episteme I (rank_k=320, proj_rank=32). I'll also define `_define_hyperparameters` with the right values.\n\nThe `ResidualCopyHead` is not in `system/defaults.py`. So, I'll implement it from scratch, just copying the first four timesteps from input `x` to output `y_copy`. This seems simple.\n\nMy MLP implementation uses two hidden layers, BatchNorm, ReLU and Dropout:\n```python\n        # First hidden layer\n        x = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_sizes[0])(factors_flat)\n        x = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(x) # BatchNorm on factors_flat\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(x)\n\n        # Second hidden layer\n\
  \        x = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_sizes[1])(x)\n        x = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(x)\n\n        # Output layer\n        factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(32 * k)(x) # Output layer to (32 * rank_k)\n        factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, 32, k))\n```\n\nThis ensures it's functionally identical. My wrapper `FactorizedMLPWrapper_Aethel` does the init and apply calls, managing the random keys and mutable state (BatchNorm). `create_network` creates the `FactorizedMLPWrapper_Aethel`.\n\nWith these steps, I should have a fully functional implementation of the modified model, ready for submission. The first step will be submitting the replicated Episteme I's model (with its original MLP). After that, I'll submit the second with my MLP.\n"
